  "worked_solution": "We decompose the fraction into a sum of partial fractions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2s+15}{{s}^{2}+9}=\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+9}+\\frac{15}{{s}^{2}+9}\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}\u20613t} & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{\\operatorname{sin}\u20613t} & =\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the addition and multiplication properties of the inverse Laplace transform, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{L}^{-1}{\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+9}+\\frac{15}{{s}^{2}+9}} & =2{L}^{-1}{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}}+5{L}^{-1}{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}\u20613t+5\\operatorname{sin}\u20613t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the inverse Laplace transform of $F(s)=\\frac{2s+15}{{s}^{2}+9}$ is $f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}\u20613t+5\\operatorname{sin}\u20613t.$",
